<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capetian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/987">987</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as House of Capet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Capetian dynasty was the royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>, who became king in 987. Its direct line ruled until 1328, and its cadet branches, the Valois and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>, held the French throne until 1848, while other branches ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, Portugal and elsewhere. Beginning with a small royal domain, the Capetians gradually extended royal power over the whole kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty.jpg" alt="Capetian dynasty" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_France_(Ancien).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Capetian dynasty, also called the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, is of Frankish origin. Agnatically it is a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a>, and it descends from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> through female lines. It takes its name from its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>, whose nickname Capet has an unknown meaning; one suggestion links it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> caput, head. Nineteenth-century historians applied the name to the ruling house of France and to all the male-line descendants of Hugh; it was not a contemporary practice. In 987 Hugh Capet was elected to succeed Louis V, the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a> that had ruled France for over three centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Capetian monarchy began as one of the weakest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, its direct rule confined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a>. The early Capetians associated their elder sons with them in the kingship, which established hereditary succession and turned a theoretically elective kingship into a sacral one. By the reign of Philip II Augustus, who became king in 1180, the hold was so strong that the practice was dropped. The kings extended their power slowly while the great peers of the realm lost theirs in succession crises. The senior line ruled until the death of Charles IV in 1328.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv-duke-of-anjou">Charles IV</a> died without male heirs, and the French lords, who rejected the claim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> through his mother Isabella, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip of Valois</a> king. From then on the succession excluded females and claims through the female line, a rule known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a>. The crown passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a>. The Revolution abolished the monarchy in 1792 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> was executed in 1793. The Bourbons returned in 1814 and left the throne in 1830 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, the last Capetian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, who reigned until 1848.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>By tradition, younger sons and brothers of the king received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> to maintain their rank and discourage them from claiming the crown. Capetian cadets therefore aimed at foreign thrones, and the family has reigned in Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, in the grand duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Brazilian empires. Cadet branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a> still reign in Spain and Luxembourg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Capetian dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early <b>Capetians</b> survived by holding on to what they had.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson Hugh Capet later founded the <b>Capetian dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He died a year later without an heir, and the nobles elected Hugh Capet, founder of the <b>Capetian dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The county lay on the frontier between Normandy, Maine and the royal domain, and its counts balanced between the Anglo-Norman kings and the <b>Capetians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perche">County of Perche</a></li></ul></section>
